1. What is an Ansible Role?

An Ansible Role is a way to organize Ansible automation into a proper folder structure.

When we start learning Ansible, we usually put everything inside one playbook:

Install packages
Copy configuration files
Create users
Start services
Create directories
Configure applications

This is okay for small labs.

But when the project becomes bigger, the playbook can become very long and difficult to manage.

Roles solve this problem by dividing the automation into separate folders and files.

For example:

Install Apache
        ↓
    apache role


Install MySQL
        ↓
    mysql role


Configure Nginx
        ↓
    nginx role

Each role handles one particular job.

2. Why do we use Roles?

Roles make Ansible code:

Organized
Reusable
Easy to understand
Easy to maintain
Easy to troubleshoot

For example, instead of having one huge playbook:

site.yml
 ├── Install Apache
 ├── Configure Apache
 ├── Copy HTML
 ├── Start Apache
 ├── Install MySQL
 ├── Configure MySQL
 └── Create MySQL users

We can separate it:

site.yml


roles/
├── apache/
└── mysql/

Now Apache-related tasks are inside the apache role and MySQL-related tasks are inside the mysql role.

3. Basic Structure of an Ansible Role

When we create a role, Ansible provides a standard directory structure.


Example:

roles/
└── apache/
    ├── tasks/
    ├── handlers/
    ├── templates/
    ├── files/
    ├── vars/
    ├── defaults/
    ├── meta/
    └── README.md

Each directory has a specific purpose.

4. tasks/

This is one of the most important directories in a role.

It contains the actual tasks that Ansible should execute.

Example:

roles/
└── apache/
    └── tasks/
        └── main.yml

main.yml could contain:

- name: Install Apache
  apt:
    name: apache2
    state: present


- name: Start Apache
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: started

So:

tasks/main.yml = What should Ansible do?

5. handlers/

Handlers are used for tasks that should run only when they are notified by another task.

A common example is restarting a service after changing its configuration.

For example:

tasks/main.yml

changes an Apache configuration.

Then it can notify:

handlers/main.yml

which contains:

- name: Restart Apache
  service:
    name: apache2
    state: restarted

Simple understanding:

Configuration changed
        ↓
Notify handler
        ↓
Restart Apache

So:

handlers/ = Special actions triggered by tasks.

6. templates/

Normally, a configuration file may contain a fixed value:

server_name = web1.example.com

But what if you have 3 servers?

web1.example.com
web2.example.com
web3.example.com

Instead of creating 3 separate configuration files, we create one template:

server_name = {{ server_name }}

Ansible will replace it with the actual value.

Here {{server_name}} is a variable which is defined in vars folder.

Example

Template file:

templates/app.conf.j2

Content:

server_name = {{ server_name }}
port = {{ app_port }}

=> Why do we use templates?

Because the same configuration file can be used on different servers with different values.

7. files/

The files directory contains files that need to be copied to the managed servers.

For example:

files/
└── index.html

Ansible can copy this file to the remote server.

Example:

- name: Copy website
  copy:
    src: index.html
    dest: /var/www/html/index.html

So:

files/ = Static files that need to be copied

8. vars/

The vars directory contains variables used by the role.

Example:

vars/
└── main.yml

It may contain:

apache_port: 80
apache_package: apache2
server_name: web1.example.com

These variables can then be used in the role.

9. defaults/

defaults/ contains default values for variables used by the role.

For example, your Apache role may have:

roles/
└── apache/
    └── defaults/
        └── main.yml

Inside main.yml:

apache_port: 80

This means:

"If nobody gives me another value, use port 80."

So 80 is the default value.

10. meta/

The meta directory contains information about the role.

It can be used to define things such as:

Role dependencies
Information about the role
Supported platforms

For example, one role may depend on another role.

Application role
       ↓
Database role

11. Role Structure:

roles/
└── apache/
    ├── tasks/
    │   └── main.yml
    │
    ├── handlers/
    │   └── main.yml
    │
    ├── templates/
    │
    ├── files/
    │
    ├── vars/
    │   └── main.yml
    │
    ├── defaults/
    │   └── main.yml
    │
    ├── meta/
    │   └── main.yml
    │
    └── README.md

12. How Roles Work with a Playbook

A role does not replace the playbook.

Instead, the playbook calls the role.

For example:

Playbook
   ↓
Apache Role
   ↓
tasks/main.yml
   ↓
Install Apache
Start Apache
Configure Apache

The playbook can look like:

- name: Configure Web Server
  hosts: web
  become: true


  roles:
    - apache

Here:

roles:
  - apache

means:

"Run the Apache role for these hosts."

13. Role vs Playbook

This is important for interviews.

a) Playbook

A playbook defines:

Which hosts should be managed
Which roles/tasks should be used

Example:

- hosts: web
  roles:
    - apache

b) Role

A role contains the actual reusable automation.

Example:

apache/
├── tasks/
├── handlers/
├── templates/
├── files/
└── defaults/

14. Easy Way to Remember

Remember this simple flow:

Inventory
   ↓
Playbook
   ↓
Role
   ↓
Tasks
   ↓
Modules
   ↓
Managed Server

=> For example:

inventory
   ↓
webservers
   ↓
site.yml
   ↓
apache role
   ↓
tasks/main.yml
   ↓
apt + service modules
   ↓
Ubuntu Server
In one sentence:

Ansible Role is a standard way of organizing related tasks, variables, files, templates and handlers into a reusable structure.
